  "answer_text": "Yes, parathyroid disease **can** cause neck pain, along with a wide range of other symptoms. Here is a detailed breakdown:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd34 Direct Neck-Related Symptoms\n\n- A common symptom of parathyroid disease is a **lump in the neck**, as parathyroid tumors can grow anywhere along the thyroid.\n- Later stages can cause tumors that are large enough to be felt during daily activities like swallowing or talking.\n- A 45-year-old woman who presented with **non-specific neck and shoulder pain** was found to have mild hypercalcemia and markedly elevated parathyroid hormone levels, along with an irregular parathyroid gland on imaging. This illustrates that neck and shoulder pain can be a presenting symptom of parathyroid disease.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\uddb4 Bone & Muscle Pain (Including the Neck Region)\n\n- The most common symptoms of hyperparathyroidism include **chronic fatigue, body aches, difficulty sleeping, bone pain**, memory loss, poor concentration, depression, and headaches.\n- PTH controls calcium levels by acting on the kidneys and releasing calcium stored in bones. When one or more parathyroid glands make too much hormone \u2014 a condition known as **primary hyperparathyroidism** \u2014 calcium is pulled out of the bones and dumped into the blood, causing a number of problems.\n- The resulting **osteoporosis** can lead to broken bones and fractures. If one of these fractures happens in the spine or neck (which are particularly fragile areas), it could cause significant pain and complications.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\ude7a Other Common Symptoms of Parathyroid Disease\n\n- Parathyroid disease also frequently leads to **osteoporosis, kidney stones, hypertension, cardiac arrhythmias, and kidney failure**.\n- **Kidney stones** are another common complication of parathyroid disease. These stones form when calcium, phosphorus, and other minerals in the urine combine to form crystals, which can cause pain and swelling if they get stuck in the kidney.\n- Other signs and symptoms can include **pain in the abdomen, side, or back** that doesn't go away, as well as bone pain.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udca1 Key Takeaway\n\nNeck pain in parathyroid disease can result from **a growing tumor/lump in the neck**, **bone loss in the spine or neck vertebrae due to hypercalcemia**, or general **body aches and bone pain** associated with hyperparathyroidism. Hyperparathyroidism is a serious disease that becomes very destructive with time, and can lead to widespread problems including osteoporosis, high blood pressure, kidney stones, kidney failure, stroke, and cardiac arrhythmias.\n\nIf you are experiencing neck pain and suspect parathyroid disease, it is important to consult a doctor who can evaluate your calcium and PTH levels.",
